] [17592186435712 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435712 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435712 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435712 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435712 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435712 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186435705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435712 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c1cb7679-3960-5f99-8d7f-3247916b22bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435713 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435713 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435713 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435713 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435713 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435713 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435713 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435713 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186435698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435713 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c7ff387b-156b-5349-95c8-a5ca6f287ec2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435714 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435714 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435714 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435714 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186435704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435714 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dff647f1-983f-5ba6-9928-87a2fdcf179e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435715 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435715 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64649" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435715 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435715 #edb/ref 1071 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435715 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0e005edd-92bb-566b-a438-96ee47079685" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435715 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=25\\,⟹\\,\\omega =5.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency $p$ of the external periodic force $f(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt$ coincides with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =5.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435716 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435716 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343698" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435716 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435716 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle of mass $0.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force \n\n$$\nF(t)=2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435716 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "189994a2-e868-5656-b67a-5717b59ea689" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435716 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.5{y}^{″}=-ky+2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2ky=4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2k\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)=2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {3}^{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435717 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435717 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64648" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435717 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435717 #edb/ref 1071 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt-\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435717 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "357bba82-a114-59bb-9168-662c1a5b596f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435717 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency $p$ of the external periodic force $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt-\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt$ coincides with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =2.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435718 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435718 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343770" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435718 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435718 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{1}{5}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{1}{5}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+\\frac{1}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435718 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d091e55-28a6-57e1-bec5-20aeba4179e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435718 #edb/ref 1074 "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $5.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =5.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =5$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(10D-25Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-(10C+25Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)]+25t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ 10D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-10C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=\\frac{4}{5}$ and $D=\\frac{1}{5}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{1}{5}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{1}{5}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{1}{5}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{1}{5}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$\n\nOnly solutions I and II match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435719 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435719 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343673" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435719 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435719 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle of mass $0.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t)=-4sin\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435719 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e7130a2f-a2af-518c-8ac7-89bf1a4dc82f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435719 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.5{y}^{″}=-ky-4sin\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2ky=-8sin\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2k\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=-8sin\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t),$ which is $\\frac{3}{2},$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{9}{8}\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435720 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435720 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343755" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435720 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435720 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{7}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{7}{4}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{7}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435720 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f5e9f5eb-87cf-56db-acd3-a7af3f0549dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435720 #edb/ref 1074 "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $2.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =2.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =2$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[4(D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4(C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)]+4t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 4D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=\\frac{7}{4}$ and $D=0.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{7}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{7}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{7}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{7}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\n\nOnly solutions I and III match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
